From: MediaBox
Subject: vd_lavc: let zero-copy decoding open its own device

This is what kept hardware decoding out of reach on this board.

The copy path creates the decoder's hardware device itself. The zero-copy
path instead demands one from the video output -- and the output has no
reason to own it: the frames come out as DRM PRIME, so the output provides
a DRM device, while the decoder is driven through an MPP device. The types
did not match, mpv said 'Could not create device' at verbose level only,
and fell back to software: 33% of eight cores instead of 1%.

Create it here when the output has none. The frames are still handed over
without a copy; only the handle the decoder is driven through is ours.
--- /var/tmp/vd_lavc.c.orig	2026-09-14 01:42:56.212523307 +0300
+++ video/decode/vd_lavc.c	2026-09-14 01:42:56.245856270 +0300
@@ -489,6 +489,27 @@
             return av_buffer_ref(hw_ctx->av_device_ref);
     }
 
+    /*
+     * The video output has no device of the type this decoder wants.
+     *
+     * That is the normal case when the decoder and the display speak
+     * different languages about the same buffer: Rockchip MPP decodes into a
+     * DRM PRIME frame, so the output provides a DRM device, while the decoder
+     * itself is driven through an MPP device. Requiring the output to hand
+     * over a device it has no reason to own is what made zero-copy decoding
+     * unreachable on this board -- the copy path worked only because it
+     * creates the device itself, a few lines above.
+     *
+     * Create it here too. The frames still come out as DRM PRIME and are
+     * still handed to the display without a copy; only the handle the decoder
+     * is driven through is opened by us rather than by the output.
+     */
+    {
+        AVBufferRef *ref = NULL;
+        if (av_hwdevice_ctx_create(&ref, hwdec->lavc_device, NULL, NULL, 0) >= 0)
+            return ref;
+    }
+
     return NULL;
 }
